<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#village-accountant">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Village accountant</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as patwari, karanam, talati, lekhpal, patowary, Shanbogue</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A village accountant, known among other titles as patwari, karanam, talati or lekhpal, is a government official in rural areas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> who keeps land and agricultural records and collects taxes. Introduced in the early 16th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> and improved by the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, the office was kept by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> with some administrative changes. The 1882 Gazetteer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Presidency describes the talati as in charge of eight to ten villages for a yearly salary of Rs. 120–180. The official closest to the villagers, the accountant reports to the tehsildar and prepares the village land records, the jamabandi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the countryside it relied on Hindu subordinate elites, such as the rawats and the <b>patwaris</b>, to collect the land revenue, and the Tughluqs employed many Hindus in their corps of architects and builders.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
